APS-010 — Policy Compliance

Document ID: APS-010
Title: Policy Compliance
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Compliance Model (CPCM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Policy Compliance Model establishes the principles, processes, evidence, and governance required to assess whether Canonical Policy Objects conform to applicable Atlas specifications, governance principles, organizational policies, contractual obligations, and regulatory requirements.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Policy compliance architecture
Compliance assessments
Compliance obligations
Compliance evidence
Exception handling
Continuous compliance
Compliance reporting
Compliance governance

This specification applies to every Canonical Policy Object.

3. Canonical Definition

Policy Compliance is the demonstrable satisfaction of all applicable governance, engineering, organizational, contractual, and regulatory obligations by a Canonical Policy Object, supported by verifiable evidence.

Policy Compliance evaluates the policy itself.

It does not evaluate runtime enforcement.

4. Objectives

The Policy Compliance Model SHALL:

Verify policy quality.
Preserve governance integrity.
Support regulatory readiness.
Enable objective assessment.
Preserve evidence.
Enable continuous monitoring.
Support certification.
Maintain traceability.
5. Compliance Principles

Every Policy Compliance assessment SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Evidence-Based

Compliance SHALL be demonstrated through objective evidence.

Independence

Compliance assessment SHALL remain independent of policy authorship.

Repeatability

Equivalent assessments SHALL produce equivalent results.

Explainability

Compliance outcomes SHALL identify the applicable obligations and supporting evidence.

Traceability

Every compliance assessment SHALL preserve complete traceability.

Continuous Assessment

Compliance SHALL be periodically reassessed throughout the policy lifecycle.

6. Compliance Sources

Policy compliance MAY be evaluated against:

Source	Example
Atlas Specifications	ACC, AHS, ADS, APS
Organizational Governance	Internal governance framework
Engineering Standards	ISO, IEEE, BIM, etc.
Regulatory Requirements	National and regional regulations
Contractual Obligations	Client-specific governance
Domain Policies	Engineering discipline requirements

Multiple compliance sources MAY apply simultaneously.

7. Compliance Assessment

Every assessment SHALL define:

Property	Description
Assessment ID	Unique identifier
Policy Reference	Evaluated policy
Applicable Obligations	Compliance requirements
Assessment Method	Manual, automated, or hybrid
Assessment Authority	Responsible assessor
Assessment Date	Evaluation timestamp
Assessment Outcome	Compliance result

Assessments SHALL be versioned.

8. Compliance Evidence

Every assessment SHALL preserve:

Policy version
Assessment methodology
Validation results
Review records
Approval records
Supporting documents
Applicable standards
Audit references

Evidence SHALL remain immutable.

9. Compliance Status

Atlas defines the following canonical compliance states.

Status	Description
Compliant	All applicable obligations satisfied
Conditionally Compliant	Minor issues remain
Non-Compliant	Mandatory obligations not satisfied
Exempted	Approved exception granted
Pending Assessment	Assessment not yet completed

Status SHALL remain explicitly recorded.

10. Exception Management

Compliance exceptions SHALL define:

Exception identifier
Applicable obligation
Justification
Risk assessment
Approval authority
Expiration date
Mitigation plan
Review schedule

Exceptions SHALL themselves be governed Canonical Atlas Objects.

11. Continuous Compliance

Policy Compliance SHALL support continuous monitoring.

Monitoring MAY detect:

Governance violations
Outdated policy references
Expired standards
Missing approvals
Regulatory changes
Policy conflicts
Lifecycle inconsistencies
Security issues

Detected issues SHALL generate Canonical Events.

12. Compliance Reporting

Every compliance report SHALL include:

Policy assessed
Assessment scope
Applicable obligations
Compliance outcome
Supporting evidence
Exceptions
Outstanding actions
Recommendations

Reports SHALL be reproducible.

13. Compliance Governance

Every compliance assessment SHALL define:

Assessment authority
Review authority
Approval authority
Audit authority
Applicable governance policies
Retention policy

Governance SHALL remain independent of assessment implementation.

14. Compliance Quality

Every compliance assessment SHALL satisfy:

Accuracy
Objectivity
Completeness
Traceability
Explainability
Repeatability
Auditability
Timeliness

Quality SHALL be continuously evaluated.

15. Compliance Events

Compliance activities MAY generate Canonical Events.

Examples include:

Compliance Assessment Started
Compliance Verified
Compliance Failed
Exception Granted
Compliance Restored
Compliance Expired

Events SHALL conform to ADS-006 — Canonical Event (CEV).

16. Conformance

A Policy Compliance implementation conforms if it:

Performs governed compliance assessments.
Preserves immutable evidence.
Supports continuous compliance monitoring.
Implements exception management.
Produces standardized reports.
Maintains traceability.
Supports audit readiness.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted compliance verification.
Continuous regulatory synchronization.
Predictive compliance analytics.
Federated compliance assessment.
Machine-readable legislation.
Autonomous compliance monitoring.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical compliance semantics.

